(a) In this section:
Portable electronic device includes, but is not limited to, MP3 or other hand-held music players, electronic reading devices, laptop computers, smart-watches, pagers, broadband personal communication devices, global positioning or navigation systems, electronic game devices, portable computing devices, or personal digital assistants.
Hands-free use means the use of a mobile telephone or portable electronic device without the use of either hand by employing an internal feature of, or an attachment to, the device.
Use means employing a handheld mobile telephone or portable electronic device for any reason including, without limitation, the following activities without employing hands-free features:
(1) Dialing a phone call;
(2) Speaking in or listening to a conversation;
(3) Viewing, taking, or transmitting electronic images;
(4) Composing, sending, viewing, accessing, browsing, retrieving, or saving email messages, text messages, or other electronic data;
(5) Entering or changing information in a global positioning or navigation system or any software or application designed for navigation;
(6) Accessing or viewing an internet website or computer application or;
(7) Playing a game.
Operating means being in actual physical control of a motor vehicle on a highway or street and includes being temporarily stopped because of traffic, a traffic light or stop sign or otherwise, but excludes operating a motor vehicle when the vehicle has pulled over to the side of the road or off an active roadway and has stopped at a location in which it can safely remain stationary.
(b) No person shall, except as otherwise provided in this section, use a mobile telephone or portable electronic device while operating a motor vehicle upon a street or highway, unless that device is configured to allow hands-free use and used in that manner while operating a motor vehicle.
(c) This section shall not apply to the following:
(1) The use of a mobile telephone or portable electronic device for the sole purpose of communicating with any of the following regarding an immediate emergency situation:
a. An emergency response operator;
b. An ambulance company;
c. Fire district and rescue service personnel;
d. Law enforcement personnel;
e. A hospital; or
f. A physician's office or health clinic.
(2) Any medical monitoring device.
(3) Activating or deactivating a mobile telephone or portable electronic device or a function of the device as long as the mobile telephone or portable electronic device is securely configured and attached to the vehicle or integrated into the vehicle.
(4) First responder exception. This section does not apply to an emergency service professional using a mobile telephone or portable electronic device while operating an emergency vehicle in the course and scope of his or her duties.
(d) An offense under this section shall be subject to section 1-8 "general penalty" provision of this Code except that the maximum fine shall not exceed $150.00. The provisions of Chapter 11 of Title 17 and any other provision of law to the contrary notwithstanding, the costs of such prosecution shall not be taxed nor shall any additional penalty, fee, or surcharge to a fine for such offense be assessed against a person for conviction thereof. Any violation of this Code section shall constitute a separate offense.
(e) This section shall be effective beginning April 2, 2018 and shall remain effective until May 31, 2020 unless sooner repealed.
(Ord. No. 2017-15, Pt. 1, 1-2-18)

